  <Ticker items={tickerItems} label="What we work with" />

  <!-- The premise -->
  <section class="section split c-wide-narrow" style="align-items:center">
    <div class="col gap-28">
      <p class="eyebrow">The premise</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif h2-lg">The past does not stay in the past simply because you have <em>moved on.</em></h2>
      <p class="body">Most people who bring spiritual hurt into therapy have already tried to sort it out on their own, usually for a long time. They have read the books. They have had the late conversations with a friend. They have prayed about it, or stopped praying about it, and neither one settled it. You may have changed your mind about what you believe, moved away from the people or places that hurt you, or built a life that looks very different from the one you grew up in. Yet certain moments may still bring a familiar fear, shame, or sense of being watched. You may find yourself reacting to authority, conflict, desire, or disappointment in ways that feel larger than the present moment.</p>
      <p class="body">This is not a sign that you have failed to move on. It reflects how deeply formative religious experiences can be, especially when they were tied to belonging, love, family, safety, or the fear of losing them.</p>
      <p class="body">Therapy can offer a place to look at these experiences without pressure to preserve, reject, explain, or resolve them. Rather than asking you to take a position, we work to understand what these experiences asked of you, what they cost, and how they may still be shaping your relationships, choices, and life now.</p>
    </div>
    <svg class="premise-figure rt-premise-art" viewBox="0 0 445 422" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Nested arcs rising from a row of points, suggesting experiences that branch and connect across a life">
