  <!-- Who this is for -->
  <section class="section sage on-color">
    <div class="stack gap-20" style="margin-bottom:48px">
      <p class="eyebrow on-color">Who I work with</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif" style="max-width:760px">Whether you stayed, left, or are still figuring out <em>what remains.</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="process-grid cols-2">
      {whoThisIsFor.map((c, i) => (
        <div class="process-card">
          <span class="num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div class="title">{c.title}</div>
          {c.copy.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="serif" style="max-width:900px; margin-top:64px; font-style:italic; font-size:clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 30px); line-height:1.45; color:var(--cream)">You do not have to know exactly where you stand before we begin. Many people hold more than one of these experiences at once.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- What the work looks like -->
  <section class="section split c-narrow-wide">
    <div class="col gap-20">
      <p class="eyebrow">The work</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif">The question is not only what you were taught, <em>but what it came to mean.</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose prose-15">
      <p class="body">The same words can take on very different meanings depending on who spoke them, when you heard them, and what was at stake in believing them. A teaching about obedience, forgiveness, purity, sacrifice, or submission may have become intertwined with love, approval, safety, or the fear of losing them.</p>
      <p class="body">This is why simply revisiting beliefs often does not resolve their impact. Rather than debating whether a belief was right or wrong in the abstract, we pay attention to how it was lived and received in your particular life. Part of this work is to understand what you learned to do with your feelings, needs, anger, body, and wanting in order to remain connected to the people and communities that mattered to you.</p>
      <p class="body">There is no prescribed conclusion in this work. You may remain deeply connected to your faith, find your relationship to it changing, step away, or simply live with questions that do not need immediate resolution. The work is to help you understand what has shaped you and live with greater freedom, honesty, and connection to what matters most to you.</p>
      <p class="body">More on <a href="/approach/" style="color:var(--blue); border-bottom:1px solid var(--blue)">how I work</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Training and background -->
  <section class="section ink on-color split c-media">
    <Portrait src="/nikole_sparks-mft-website.jpg" alt="Nikole Sparks" placeholder="Nikole — candid photo" />
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow on-color">Background</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif h2-lg">Faith and its impact can <em>both be taken seriously.</em></h2>
      <p class="body">Religious hurt or trauma is not just a category. It often involves a language, community, set of practices, and way of making meaning that have mattered deeply. You do not have to spend our sessions translating the theological language, meaning, or questions that have shaped your life.</p>
      <p class="body">My background includes a master’s degree in Spiritual Formation and Soul Care from Talbot School of Theology, training in spiritual direction, and ongoing postgraduate psychoanalytic training at the Newport Psychoanalytic Institute. Together, these inform a way of working that is theologically literate and psychologically attentive.</p>
      <p class="body">That combination allows us to take both faith and its impact seriously. You do not need to defend what was meaningful to you, minimize what hurt, or arrive having decided what you believe now. We can begin with your story and make room for its complexity.</p>
      <a class="text-link on-color" href="/about/">About Nikole <span class="arw">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQs -->
  <section class="section" style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr; gap:80px; align-items:start">
    <div class="sticky-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Religious trauma</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <Illustration name="questions-full" size="240px" class="mt-16" />
    </div>
    <Faq items={faqs} />
  </section>

  <!-- The bridge to faith-integrated therapy -->
  <section class="section sage on-color">
    <div class="stack gap-20" style="max-width:760px">
      <p class="eyebrow on-color">A companion path</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif">If faith is still the thing <em>that holds.</em></h2>
      <p class="body">Religion is rarely all one thing. Some of what you were given may have cost you and some of it may still be carrying you, and those can be true in the same person on the same Sunday.</p>
      <p class="body">If the sustaining part is the larger part right now, and what you are looking for is therapy that takes your spiritual life seriously rather than therapy about what went wrong in it, <a href="/services/faith-integrated-therapy/" style="color:var(--cream); border-bottom:1px solid var(--cream-35)">faith-integrated therapy</a> describes that work. It is the same practice and the same clinician. You do not need to have sorted out which page you belong on before you call, and most people do not arrive knowing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <NextSteps
    heading="Ready to stop carrying this alone? <em>Let’s talk.</em>"
    copy="The free 15-minute consultation is a conversation, not an intake. You can tell me as much or as little of the history as you want to. In-person in Newport Beach and Fullerton, or telehealth throughout California."
  />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Center the premise illustration within its narrow column so it sits
     balanced rather than hugging the left edge. */
  .premise-figure {
    justify-self: center;
    align-self: start;
  }
  /* Custom inline premise SVG: sized to match the other premise
     illustrations across the site. */
  .rt-premise-art {
    width: clamp(260px, 30vw, 380px);
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }
  /* Hero flourishing SVG: the source art has heavy internal padding, so the
     viewBox is cropped to the bloom to fill the hero column. The stroke is set
     thin so it renders at the site's ~1.25px hero line weight at that crop. */
  .rt-hero-art ellipse {
    stroke-width: 0.7;
  }
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    section[style*='grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr'] {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
      gap: 40px !important;
    }
  }
</style>
